<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 9 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">Voyagers of the Pacific</h1><p class="path-tagline">Canoes, stars and sacred kings: the peoples who settled the widest ocean on Earth, and what happened when Europe arrived.</p><p class="path-intro">The Pacific was settled long before it was mapped. Descendants of Austronesian seafarers sailed east across open ocean, reading stars, winds and swells, until Polynesians had reached Hawaii, New Zealand and Rapa Nui, the corners of a triangle thousands of kilometres across. On the islands they built societies of their own: a sacred line of kings on Tonga, a basalt city in the lagoon of Pohnpei, the moai of Easter Island. This path follows them from the first voyages to the encounters with European ships in the eighteenth century, and on to the kingdoms that rose, fell or, in Tonga's case, endured.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Navigator</a> · <a href="/start">the Chronicler</a></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/polynesians"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polynesians.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Encyclopedia · People</span><span class="path-step-name">Polynesians</span><span class="path-step-why">Descended from Austronesian seafarers of the Lapita culture, they reached Tonga and Samoa around 800 BCE and later found the remotest islands by stars, winds and swells.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/tu-i-tonga-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haamonga-a-maui.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Profile · 950 CE – 1865 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Tuʻi Tonga Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">A line of sacred kings at Muʻa on Tongatapu whose voyaging and tribute networks reached ʻUvea, Samoa and eastern Fiji.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/saudeleur-dynasty"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/temwen-island.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Profile · 1100 CE – 1628 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Saudeleur dynasty</span><span class="path-step-why">The first rulers of all Pohnpei governed from Nan Madol, nearly a hundred artificial islets of stacked basalt in a coral lagoon.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/rapa-nui"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rano-raraku.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Profile · 1200 CE – 1888 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Rapa Nui</span><span class="path-step-why">Settled around 1200 on one of the remotest inhabited islands on Earth, its people carved some 887 moai and devised rongorongo, the only possible script known from Polynesia.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/tupaia"><span class="path-step-ph">5</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Encyclopedia · Person · c. 1725–1770</span><span class="path-step-name">Tupaia</span><span class="path-step-why">The Raiatean priest and navigator who joined Cook's Endeavour in 1769, drew a chart of the islands Tahitians knew and spoke for Cook with the Māori.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-cook.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1728–1779</span><span class="path-step-name">James Cook</span><span class="path-step-why">Three voyages between 1768 and 1779 charted New Zealand and eastern Australia; the last ended with his death in a clash at Kealakekua Bay on Hawaii Island.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/kingdom-of-hawaii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iolani-palace.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Profile · 1795 CE – 1893 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Kingdom of Hawaii</span><span class="path-step-why">United by Kamehameha I by 1810 and recognised by Britain and France in 1843, it was overthrown in 1893 by businessmen backed by US troops.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-waitangi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-waitangi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1840</span><span class="path-step-name">Treaty of Waitangi</span><span class="path-step-why">Signed in 1840 by the British Crown and Māori chiefs, in English and Māori texts that differ on sovereignty: a dispute the Waitangi Tribunal has heard since 1975.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/kingdom-of-tonga"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-tupou-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Profile · 1845 CE – 2025 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Kingdom of Tonga</span><span class="path-step-why">The only Pacific island state never formally colonised: the kingdom George Tupou I founded in 1845, whose constitution of 1875 is still in force.</span></span></a></li></ol>
